  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 3,
    "lesson_title": "Frequency Spacing in Fourier Series",
    "lesson_core_move": "Compute the Fourier-series frequency spacing $\\omega$_0 = 2*pi/T and interpret how it shrinks toward continuous frequency as the period grows without bound.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Frequency Spacing in Fourier Series",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Frequency Spacing in Fourier Series",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In a Fourier series, the spectrum is not continuous. It appears as discrete harmonic lines, and the spacing between neighboring lines is the angular-frequency spacing $\\omega_0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega_0 = \\frac{2\\pi}{T}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So if you know the period $T$, you know the gap between adjacent Fourier-series lines. The $n$th harmonic sits at $\\omega_n = n\\omega_0$, so every line is an integer multiple of the same spacing.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega_n = n\\omega_0 = \\frac{2\\pi n}{T}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This gives the key trend: when $T$ gets larger, $\\omega_0$ gets smaller. The same harmonic numbers are still there, but the lines are packed more tightly together.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\lim_{T\\to\\infty} \\omega_0 = 0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That is the bridge to continuous frequency: as $T\\to\\infty$, the spacing collapses toward zero, and the Fourier-series line spectrum becomes arbitrarily dense.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Frequency Spacing in Fourier Series",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Computing Frequency Spacing from the Period",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A periodic signal has period $T=0.5$ s. Compute the Fourier-series spacing $\\omega_0$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the period $T$. For Fourier-series line spacing, use $\\omega_0 = 2\\pi/T$ and substitute the given period directly.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega_0 = \\frac{2\\pi}{T} = \\frac{2\\pi}{0.5} = 4\\pi",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the adjacent Fourier-series harmonics are separated by $4\\pi$ rad/s. That is the harmonic gap for this signal, and it is the quantity that will shrink when the period grows.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Frequency Spacing in Fourier Series",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Finding Harmonic Frequencies from the Spacing",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For a signal with period $T=2$ s, compute $\\omega_0$ and then list $\\omega_1$, $\\omega_2$, and $\\omega_3$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that once the spacing is known, each harmonic frequency is just an integer multiple of $\\omega_0$. For $T=2$ s, first compute the spacing from the period.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega_0 = \\frac{2\\pi}{T} = \\frac{2\\pi}{2} = \\pi",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now use the harmonic rule $\\omega_n = n\\omega_0$. That means the first three positive harmonic lines are the 1st, 2nd, and 3rd multiples of the same spacing.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega_1 = \\pi,\\quad \\omega_2 = 2\\pi,\\quad \\omega_3 = 3\\pi",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the harmonics stay equally spaced by $\\omega_0 = \\pi$ rad/s, and the full line pattern is just integer multiples of that gap.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Frequency Spacing in Fourier Series",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Comparing Frequency Spacing for Larger Periods",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Compare the spacing for periods $T$ and $3T$ and identify which harmonic spectrum is denser.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a period change by a factor of $3$, so the spacing rule $\\omega_0 = 2\\pi/T$ is the only thing you need. Start from the baseline period $T$ and then replace $T$ by $3T$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega_0 = \\frac{2\\pi}{T},\\qquad \\omega_0' = \\frac{2\\pi}{3T} = \\frac{1}{3}\\omega_0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the larger period gives one-third the spacing. The harmonic lines move closer together, which means the spectrum is denser.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\frac{\\omega_0'}{\\omega_0} = \\frac{1}{3}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the reusable inverse scaling rule: multiply the period by a factor, and the Fourier-series spacing shrinks by the same factor.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Frequency Spacing in Fourier Series",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Interpreting Frequency Spacing as the Period Grows Without Bound",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "If the period grows without bound, what happens to $\\omega_0 = 2\\pi/T$ and the spacing between harmonic lines?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the limit $T\\to\\infty$. Use the same spacing rule $\\omega_0 = 2\\pi/T$ and watch what happens as the denominator grows.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\lim_{T\\to\\infty} \\omega_0 = \\lim_{T\\to\\infty} \\frac{2\\pi}{T} = 0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the harmonic spacing collapses to zero. The Fourier-series lines crowd together, which is the signal that the spectrum is approaching a continuous frequency axis.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
